{
  "image_name": "0f7ae6183e9164b85b0c70133b112965.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes bile acid metabolism across the liver, intestine, and circulation, emphasizing how host enzymes and gut microbes jointly shape the bile acid pool. In hepatocytes, cholesterol is converted through classical and alternative pathways into primary bile acids, mainly cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid, then conjugated before secretion into bile. Transporters at the canalicular and sinusoidal membranes mediate export into bile and reuptake from portal blood, illustrating enterohepatic circulation.\n\nIn the intestinal lumen, microbial communities modify these bile acids through several key reactions. Conjugated bile acids are first deconjugated by bacterial bile salt hydrolase activity. Other microbes then carry out epimerization and 7α-dehydroxylation, generating a broader range of secondary bile acids such as deoxycholic acid, lithocholic acid, and ursodeoxycholic acid. The diagram also highlights muricholic acid species and their conversion into related derivatives, suggesting a prominent role for these compounds in this system.\n\nOverall, the image shows a dynamic loop: the liver synthesizes and conjugates bile acids, the intestine and microbiota transform them, and transporters return them to the liver through the portal vein and systemic circulation.",
